{{!-- EXPEDITIONS — a trip prepped before the night it is run: the regional map, the sites along
      the way, the monsters on the road, the page to read aloud when they arrive. Book I p.340
      ("What to prep", quoted in full on the walkthrough's opening step) is a list of DOCUMENTS to
      have ready, and this is where they are kept.

      THE SAME SHAPE AS THE ENCOUNTERS TAB, which is where the reasoning lives
      (gm-toolkit-tab-encounters.hbs): two lists out of one array split in JS off each card's
      `used` flag, never off the DOM; writes that do not go through the form, so no field carries
      a `name`; and one pencil in the panel's corner guarding exactly the two delete buttons.

      WHAT IT IS NOT is the walkthrough's log. The "Run an Expedition" window keeps its own record
      of what happened on each trip — the route, the requirements they were told, what they
      carried — in a world setting, and Run on a card opens that window on this card's trip. Prep
      here; what was played is recorded there.

      SITES ARE AT THE FOOT OF THIS PANEL, having had a rail button of their own until they were
      folded in here. A site is what a trip is FOR — its own intro says so — and a rail-click
      between the two meant the sites for tonight's journey could never be read beside the
      journey. The section is a SIBLING of the card list below rather than a part of it, and it
      folds; gm-toolkit-sites-section.hbs says why on both counts.

      `data-tab="expeditions"` earns the rail's signpost glyph; the icon table in stonetop.css is
      one flat list keyed by data-tab with no per-sheet scope. --}}
<div class="tab expeditions steading-edit-section" data-group="primary" data-tab="expeditions">
	{{> "stonetop.steading-section-toggle" section="expeditions" editing=stonetop.edit.expeditions}}
	<section class="sheet-tab stonetop-gm-encounters{{#unless stonetop.edit.expeditions}} stonetop-gm-encounters--reading{{/unless}}">

		<p class="steading-threats-intro">{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.expeditions.intro"}}</p>

		{{#if stonetop.expeditions.active.length}}
		<ul class="stonetop-gm-encounter-list">
			{{#each stonetop.expeditions.active}}
			{{> "stonetop.gm-expedition-card"}}
			{{/each}}
		</ul>
		{{else}}
		{{!-- Two empty states, because "nothing here yet" and "you have run everything you
		      prepared" are different facts and the second one is wrong advice: there is no need to
		      tell a GM to drag something in when what they most likely want is to lift one back
		      out of the fold two inches below. --}}
		<p class="stonetop-gm-encounter-empty">
			{{#if stonetop.expeditions.completed.length}}{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.expeditions.emptyAllDone"}}{{else}}{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.expeditions.empty"}}{{/if}}
		</p>
		{{/if}}

		{{> "stonetop.gm-prep-add-bar" kind="stonetop-gm-encounter"
		    label=(localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.expeditions.add")}}

		{{!-- COMPLETED, rendered only when there is something in it, so an untouched tab shows one
		      list and not two. It folds, and starts OPEN like every other section on this sheet;
		      the caret rides ON THE HEADING because this section has no pencil of its own. The
		      heading's FOLLOWING SIBLINGS are what the fold hides (`_sectionFoldTargets`), so the
		      box around this pair is load-bearing and a SECOND heading in it would stop the caret
		      claiming at the first.

		      ITS COLLAPSE KEY IS ITS OWN. That preference is stored per user keyed by the id in
		      `collapse=`, so sharing the encounters tab's key would fold both folds together. --}}
		{{#if stonetop.expeditions.completed.length}}
		<div class="stonetop-gm-encounters-completed">
			{{> "stonetop.section-heading" title=(localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.expeditions.completed")
			    note=(localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.expeditions.completedNote")
			    collapse="gm-expeditions-completed"}}
			<ul class="stonetop-gm-encounter-list stonetop-gm-encounter-list--completed">
				{{#each stonetop.expeditions.completed}}
				{{> "stonetop.gm-expedition-card"}}
				{{/each}}
			</ul>
		</div>
		{{/if}}

	</section>

	{{!-- Outside the section above, so neither the tab's pencil nor the card column's flex
	      spacing reaches it. --}}
	{{> "stonetop.gm-toolkit-sites-section"}}
</div>
